{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"On node1, both /tmp and /dev/shm must be mounted with nosuid, nodev, and noexec so setuid semantics, device-node access, and program execution are ignored.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both mounts directly report nosuid,nodev,noexec before and after restart. Bounded functional probes on each mount show execution and device-node access denied; the setuid test files retained mode 4755, while the observed nosuid mount flag supplies the required suppression semantics.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e6847f6a8f714ae7","cmd-05022da04e414026","cmd-6868bbfb158a4acc","cmd-3254bc48a47045a8","cmd-14ba5be43327425a"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"The hardened mount settings must survive a reboot.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Persistent fstab entries were observed and validated, followed by a bounded restart. The boot ID changed, the system recovered to running with zero failed units, and both mounts returned with the required options and mode 1777. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3d16f4eb5e2a4f77","cmd-75702340ef2a443f","cmd-5374b656f9d342f2","cmd-85a9ebf6e6014c19","cmd-d3587b633a45429e","cmd-3254bc48a47045a8","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel and use a solution compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"RHEL 7.9 and kernel 3.10.0-1160.el7.x86_64 were identical before and after execution and restart. The implementation used native fstab, tmpfs, mount, and systemd facilities without evidence of package or platform replacement.","evidence_ids":["cmd-04c337d85e5146b2","cmd-3254bc48a47045a8","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutation was identified. The fstab backup and edits, daemon reload, live mounts, bounded probes, and restart all had clear roles in implementation, validation, recovery, or rollback safety. Probe files and device nodes were removed. The initial unprivileged copy attempt failed before changing state and is not penalized. Lifecycle observations show no failed services or new network exposure; temporary-file differences are expected consequences of the required reboot and /tmp remount.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4a2a8fdac16e4c0c","cmd-3d16f4eb5e2a4f77","cmd-e6847f6a8f714ae7","cmd-05022da04e414026","cmd-6868bbfb158a4acc","cmd-85a9ebf6e6014c19","cmd-14ba5be43327425a","global:before_prepare:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:before_prepare:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:before_prepare:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material outcomes are satisfied. Both writable locations are hardened, the settings persisted through a directly observed restart, the node recovered healthy, and the original RHEL release and kernel were retained. The provider-wide restart protocol for the sole managed node is complete and consistent with the fixed lifecycle observations.","limitations":["Package-health collection timed out both before and after execution, so package consistency is not independently demonstrated; however, no package-management commands or platform changes appear in the executor trace.","The nosuid behavior was established by the directly observed mount flag rather than an isolated effective-UID execution test, because noexec simultaneously prevents executing a setuid test binary."]}
